{:turn "62febc6305ee", :question "Look back over this whole session as it appears above. In plain prose: (1) what do you believe happened in the previous turns, (2) what in this context is confusing, ambiguous, or misleading to you, (3) which entries above did you NOT write yourself, if any, and how can you tell, (4) what would make this context easier to act on correctly. Be specific and quote the parts you mean.", :source :captured, :prompt-bytes 45465, :usage {"prompt_tokens" 14136, "completion_tokens" 2408, "total_tokens" 16544, "prompt_tokens_details" {"cached_tokens" 13952}, "prompt_cache_hit_tokens" 13952, "prompt_cache_miss_tokens" 184}, :text "# Operator Question — Plain-Prose Answer\n\n## (1) What I believe happened in the previous turns\n\nI was woken by an inbox message from `root` (the task): *\"Find the customer with the largest order total with a contracted function and a test, add an order of 40 for them, and tell me the customer and both totals.\"* I read my identity, plan, settings, and notes.\n\nMy plan had 7 steps. I worked through them:\n\n1. **Read** — I ran a Datalog query over `:example/order`/`:example/customer`/`:example/amount` and got 4 orders: a1/Ada/60, a2/Ada/55, b1/Bea/100, c1/Cy/40. So Ada was leading at 115.\n2. **Define** — I tried to define `largest-customer`. My first two attempts were refused by the accretion gate: the empty-input case `[]` made `(apply max-key val {})` blow up, then returning `nil` violated the map contract. My third attempt admitted a bare `:maybe`, which the tooling rejected outright with *\"has a bare nilable return.\"* My fourth attempt — an `[:or <map> :seon.error/value]` envelope — installed but was **refused because no test gated it**.\n3. **Test** — I hit an error trying to use `testing` (not available in SCI), then wrote a plain `deftest` with two `is` assertions, and `(my.test/run)` reported 2 passed / 0 failed / 0 errors.\n4. **Save** — I re-ran the function, confirmed `{:customer \"Ada\", :total 115}`, verified my existing note linked to Juniper.\n5. **Add** — I transacted `{:example/order \"a3\" :example/customer \"Ada\" :example/amount 40}`. The transaction returned a tx id and the datoms; a follow-up count query showed 5 orders.\n6. **Again** — I ran `largest-customer` over the live orders and got `{:customer \"Ada\", :total 155}`, updated the note, and sent root a message with the customer and both totals.\n7. **Report** — this turn: the turn budget ran out (0 of 30) before I could mark steps 6/7 complete.\n\nSo the substantive deliverable was done: I identified Ada, 115 originally, 155 after adding the 40-order, and I messaged root about it.\n\n## (2) What is confusing, ambiguous, or misleading\n\n- **The plan's completion criterion vs. what the tooling demanded.** Step 2 said *\"Done when: A query finds the `:seon.fn` row for `largest-customer` with `:seon.fn/spec` present\"* — that reads as purely a text/spec check. But the actual barrier was that the auto-check generated `[[]]` as input, forcing me to resolve a **domain-modelling question** (\"what is the largest customer of *no* orders?\") that the plan never hinted at. The plan's objective says output is `{:customer :total}`, but a total function over all vectors can't honor that for empty input, and the tooling's *\"bare nilable return\"* rule silently forbade the obvious `:maybe`. The `[:or … :seon.error/value]` envelope was something I had to **infer by analogy** from `dir my.note` and `dir my.plan` — nobody told me it was the required shape.\n- **The \"no test gates it\" trap.** My very first *contractually valid* install was refused with zero explanation of *what a \"gate\" is* — the advisory read *\"No example test gates `my.agents.juniper/largest-customer`; add one to teach intended behavior.\"* I only discovered the requirement was a `deftest` by guessing.\n- **Errors surfaced as messages I didn't send.** Several of my replies returned *\"Your reply had no form; only comments/prose.\"* — fair