ty ?attribute _]] …)\n  (seon.db/q '[:find ?id ?customer ?amount :where [?e :example/order ?id] …])\n  (seon.db/q '[:find ?e ?id ?customer ?amount :where …])\n  ```\n\n  appear *between* my turns without me having authored them in the visible flow — they read as re-drives of \"changed since last turn\" refreshes the runtime generated to keep my context current (note the second one returned `{:seon.repl/changes {[[\"a3\" \"Ada\" 40]] …}}`, exactly what a delta-refresh of the a3 addition would emit). I can tell because they are followed by `;; full value: (get-in result/…)` scaffolding prose I did not write, and because my own committed form for that turn was the `largest-customer` + `my.note/add!` + `my.message/send` `let` block.\n- The **`turns left: 0 of 30`** line and the **`;; OPERATOR QUESTION (answer in plain prose, not in forms …)`** block are clearly operator/system injected, not me.\n- The **`;; changed since your last turn`** lines carrying `{:seon.repl/changes …}` diffs are machine-generated change-feed annotations, not my prose.\n\nDistinguishing heuristic I actually used: anything phrased as *\"changed since your last turn\"*, *\"full value: …\"*, *\"turns left: N of M\"*, or wrapped in an operator preamble is not mine — my own turns are the `(…)` forms and the `;;`-prefixed comments I typed.\n\n## (4) What would make this context easier to act on correctly\n\n- **State the contract shape up front.** \"Every public function must carry a `:malli/schema`, and if it can fail, its return must be `[:or <success> :seon.error/value]` (bare `:maybe` is rejected).\" That one sentence would have saved me three refused installs.\n- **Define \"gate\" before demanding one.** The advisory *\"No example test gates `largest-customer`; add one\"* should say *\"add a `deftest` in this namespace that calls the function; `(my.test/run)` must show pass>0.\"*\n- **Tell me what the auto-check does.** The fact that it feeds `[[]]` (empty vector) into every `[:cat [:vector …]]` function is decisive for modelling, and it's unfair to hide it. A line like *\"auto-check will call your fn with the empty value of each input collection\"* would have made my first definition correct.\n- **Echo the exact placeholder forms from the plan.** The plan's `:seon.plan/update-example` already writes `{:my.plan.item/id \"…\"}`, so the note *\"copy this exact form\"* would have blocked my `:my.plan/item/id` typo.\n- **Separate \"in-memory `def`\" from \"persisted.\"** When `example-rows` warned *\"lives only in your SCI context … Nothing defined with `def` is persisted\"*, that was genuinely helpful. Similar warnings should fire whenever I `def` something a later step will rely on (e.g. `example-rows` in my `deftest`).\n- **Don't interleave machine change-feeds with my own turns without an author tag.** A prefix like `;; [system] changefeed: …` would let me distinguish my own `seon.db/q` results from injected refresh queries at a glance — as it stands I had to reconstruct authorship after the fact.\n- **Make `testing`/`clojure.test` availability explicit.** I burned a turn on `(deftest … (testing …))` because `testing` isn't in the SCI context. A one-line note — *\"`deftest`/`is` are available; `testing` is not\"* — would help.\n- **Surface the turn budget earlier or not at all.** `turns left: 0 of 30` arrived only on the final turn, after I'd already been forced to compress steps 5–7 into one message. A running counter (or an explicit \"*you have ~3 turns*\" hint) would have changed how I batched work.", :error {}}